{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a33f7c588e5aa59c","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9018\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# RHAISTRAT-9018 Decomposition — GPU chargeback reporting for platform administrators\n11\t\n12\tStrategy status: Refinement Done. Priority: Major. External link: depends-on\n13\tRHAISTRAT-9990 (GPU usage metering — in flight, separate strategy).\n14\t\n15\t## Epic List\n16\t\n17\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n18\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n19\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E001 | Metering data consumption contract for GPU chargeback reporting | Investigation | Distributed Workloads | P0 |\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 | Per-project GPU-hour report computation backend with stable API and CSV export | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P0 |\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 | Dashboard admin view for GPU chargeback reporting and Kueue quota comparison | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P0 |\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E004 | Documentation for GPU chargeback report semantics and export integration | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n23\t\n24\t## Dependency DAG\n25\t\n26\t```mermaid\n27\tgraph TD\n28\t    E001[\"E001 consumption contract investigation\"] --> E002[\"E002 report backend with stable API and CSV export\"]\n29\t    E002 --> E003[\"E003 Dashboard admin view\"]\n30\t    E002 --> E004[\"E004 documentation\"]\n31\t    E003 --> E004\n32\t```\n33\t\n34\t## DAG Justification\n35\t\n36\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n37\t|------|------|-----------|\n38\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The strategy's open question — the metering data schema finalizes only when the in-flight metering strategy (RHAISTRAT-9990) lands — determines how the report backend consumes the data (directly vs. behind an isolation layer) and what the per-project GPU-hour attribution semantics are. True gate: `rewrite` of the backend's data-access approach. Recorded as `gated_by` on E002. |\n39\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 13 (artifact → consumer) | The Dashboard view builds against the report API produced by the backend; code builds against an API, so the consumer is serial after the producer. |\n40\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes the built reporting capability and its export integration. |\n41\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes the built administrator view and quota comparison. |\n42\t\n43\tNo conditional branch epics: the investigation's bounded outcomes (schema stable\n44\tenough to consume directly vs. consume behind an isolation layer) change the\n45\tbackend epic's approach, not which epics exist — both outcomes keep the same\n46\tepic set. The outcome is therefore recorded as `gate_failure_impact` on the\n47\tgated epic rather than as branch files.\n48\t\n49\tExternal dependency modeling (Rule 21): the metering delivery is resolved by a\n50\tthird party (the in-flight RHAISTRAT-9990 strategy), so it is modeled as a\n51\tprecondition with a risk note on the report backend epic — no separate epic,\n52\tand no re-implementation of collection (explicitly out of scope in the\n53\tstrategy).\n54\t\n55\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n56\t\n57\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Rationale |\n58\t|-----|----------|---------|-----------|\n59\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E002, E003 | Report computation over selectable time ranges (backend) plus the administrator-visible report view (Dashboard). Full coverage. |\n60\t| HLR-2 | P1 | E002 (bundled) — CSV export and the stable finance API ride the same report-delivery surface as HLR-1's report computation; a split would build the same service and endpoints twice and artificially serialize one PR review cycle over the same surface | |\n61\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E003 (bundled) — the current-month consumption-vs-quota view is a presentation mode of the same administrator view and the same per-project data as HLR-1's report list; it shares the UI surface and is not separately schedulable | |\n62\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E004 | New documentation topics (report semantics, export integration) — substantive content authoring, so a separate docs-authoring epic. Priority P1 per the docs-authoring exception (strategy priority Major → P1). |\n63\t\n64\t## Health Warnings\n65\t\n66\t- **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted\n67\t  Teams table, no per-component change/owner detail (Affected Components is a\n68\t  bare list), no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria,\n69\t  and no Non-Functional Requirements. Teams were inferred from architecture\n70\t  context (Dashboard → RHOAI Dashboard; Distributed Workloads → Distributed\n71\t  Workloads); epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs.\n72\t- **External timing risk** (strategy open question): the metering data schema\n73\t  finalizes only when RHAISTRAT-9990 lands. The timing is externally\n74\t  controlled and cannot be compressed; it is recorded as a risk on the report\n75\t  backend epic, not as an acceptance criterion.\n76\t- **Non-obvious component mapping**: the report computation backend is\n77\t  assigned to Distributed Workloads. The strategy lists only Dashboard and\n78\t  Distributed Workloads as affected, and per `dashboard.md` the Dashboard owns\n79\t  no data plane and exposes no private component APIs, so backend-side\n80\t  reporting work maps to Distributed Workloads — the component whose stack\n81\t  runs the metered GPU workloads and owns the Kueue quota model.\n82\t- **Distributed Workloads is passive for HLR-3**: per-project Kueue quota is\n83\t  readable from the existing Kueue CRDs, so no Distributed Workloads code\n84\t  change is needed for the quota side; its validation is an acceptance\n85\t  criterion on the Dashboard view epic.\n86\t- **Bundled P1 HLRs**: HLR-2 and HLR-3 are P1 but bundled into P0 epics on\n87\t  shared surfaces — the inline rationale is in the traceability matrix rows\n88\t  above.\n89\t\n90\t## Tiered Delivery\n91\t\n92\tEvaluated per Rule 18 / Step 5.5: the external dependency (metering data\n93\tschema, uncertain timing) gates every user-facing deliverable — reports, the\n94\tquota-vs-consumption view, and exports all compute from the metering data, and\n95\ta quota-only view has no independent chargeback value. No partial-value tier\n96\texists beyond the risk-reduction investigation itself, so delivery is linear:\n97\tthe consumption-contract investigation proceeds first, and the report backend,\n98\tDashboard view, and documentation follow. The metering dependency is modeled\n99\tas a precondition (Rule 21 — the third party resolves it), with the timing\n100\trisk noted in Health Warnings.\n101\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9927b2a906084768","agent_id":"a5d3bd03a73995913","session_id":"93310d5d-28f6-44a7-9cad-8975610828da","uuid":"74252908-3b0f-4e6f-9da2-aa02caaa4256","timestamp":"2026-10-26T18:13:58.112Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a33f7c588e5aa59c","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9018 decomposition"}
