ct GPU-hour attribution semantics are. True gate: `rewrite` of the backend's data-access approach. Recorded as `gated_by` on E002. |\n38\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 13 (artifact → consumer) | The Dashboard view builds against the report API produced by the backend; code builds against an API, so the consumer is serial after the producer. |\n39\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes the built reporting capability and its export integration. |\n40\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes the built administrator view and quota comparison. |\n41\t\n42\tNo conditional branch epics: the investigation's bounded outcomes (schema stable\n43\tenough to consume directly vs. consume behind an isolation layer) change the\n44\tbackend epic's approach, not which epics exist — both outcomes keep the same\n45\tepic set. The outcome is therefore recorded as `gate_failure_impact` on the\n46\tgated epic rather than as branch files.\n47\t\n48\tExternal dependency modeling (Rule 21): the metering delivery is resolved by a\n49\tthird party (the in-flight RHAISTRAT-9990 strategy), so it is modeled as a\n50\tprecondition with a risk note on the report backend epic — no separate epic,\n51\tand no re-implementation of collection (explicitly out of scope in the\n52\tstrategy).\n53\t\n54\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n55\t\n56\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Rationale |\n57\t|-----|----------|---------|-----------|\n58\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E002, E003 | Report computation over selectable time ranges (backend) plus the administrator-visible report view (Dashboard). Full coverage. |\n59\t| HLR-2 | P1 | E002 (bundled) — CSV export and the stable finance API ride the same report-delivery surface as HLR-1's report computation; a split would build the same service and endpoints twice and artificially serialize one PR review cycle over the same surface | |\n60\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E003 (bundled) — the current-month consumption-vs-quota view is a presentation mode of the same administrator view and the same per-project data as HLR-1's report list; it shares the UI surface and is not separately schedulable | |\n61\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E004 | New documentation topics (report semantics, export integration) — substantive content authoring, so a separate docs-authoring epic. Priority P1 per the docs-authoring exception (strategy priority Major → P1). |\n62\t\n63\t## Health Warnings\n64\t\n65\t- **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted\n66\t  Teams table, no per-component change/owner detail (Affected Components is a\n67\t  bare list), no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria,\n68\t  and no Non-Functional Requirements. Teams were inferred from architecture\n69\t  context (Dashboard → RHOAI Dashboard; Distributed Workloads → Distributed\n70\t  Workloads); epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs.\n71\t- **External timing risk** (strategy open question): the metering data schema\n72\t  finalizes only when RHAISTRAT-9990 lands. The timing is externally\n73\t  controlled and cannot be compressed; it is recorded as a risk on the report\n74\t  backend epic, not as an acceptance criterion.\n75\t- **Non-obvious component mapping**: the report computation backend is\n76\t  assigned to Distributed Workloads. The strategy lists only Dashboard and\n77\t  Distributed Workloads as affected, and per `dashboard.md` the Dashboard owns\n78\t  no data plane and exposes no private component APIs, so backend-side\n79\t  reporting work maps to Distributed Workloads — the component whose stack\n80\t  runs the metered GPU workloads and owns the Kueue quota model.\n81\t- **Distributed Workloads is passive for HLR-3**: per-project Kueue quota is\n82\t  readable from the existing Kueue CRDs, so no Distributed Workloads code\n83\t  change is needed for the quota side; its validation is an acceptance\n84\t  criterion on the Dashboard view epic.\n85\t- **Bundled P1 HLRs**: HLR-2 and HLR-3 are P1 but bundled into P0 epics on\n86\t  shared surfaces — the inline rationale is in the traceability matrix rows\n87\t  above.\n88\t\n89\t## Tiered Delivery\n90\t\n91\tEvaluated per Rule 18 / Step 5.5: the external dependency (metering data\n92\tschema, uncertain timing) gates every user-facing deliverable — reports, the\n93\tquota-vs-consumption view, and exports all compute from the metering data, and\n94\ta quota-only view has no independent chargeback value. No partial-value tier\n95\texists beyond the risk-reduction investigation itself, so delivery is linear:\n96\tthe consumption-contract investigation proceeds first, and the report backend,\n97\tDashboard view, and documentation follow. The metering dependency is modeled\n98\tas a precondition (Rule 21 — the third party resolves it), with the timing\n99\trisk noted in Health Warnings.\n100\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-82e0f773b786c9ec","agent_id":"ae405c91d4039ec4c","session_id":"93310d5d-28f6-44a7-9cad-8975610828da","uuid":"c3902be2-54c8-421c-b72b-884a29d0edc8","timestamp":"2026-10-26T18:19:19.429Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a04a2c87f50f7687","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9018 decomposition"}
